---
/**
 * THE OUTSIDE LINK ON AN OPENING FRAME — applied from preview/ab-zw13,
 * THE INLINE, on 2026-10-06, chosen from fourteen built as real pages.
 *
 * It replaces `.still__go`, which was a translucent pill: a 1px 50%-white
 * border, a 35% ground and a 6px backdrop-filter holding a 10px mono label.
 *
 * WHAT WENT IS THE BORDER AND THE BLUR; A WASH STAYED. ZW13's argument was
 * that the plate held white type off a brightness that is not there — true of
 * Aasha's frame, where the button's own box measures mean .0077 and p95 .0168
 * and white reads 18.2:1 and 15.7:1. It is NOT true of the catalogue: three of
 * the six frames that carry this control cannot hold unplated white type at
 * all. The full readings and the solved alpha are at `.wm__scrim` below.
 *
 * TWO TIERS OUT OF UGAM'S OWN LABEL, RE-SET AND NEVER REWRITTEN. The markdown
 * says `label: "Watch on ZEE5"` and `platform: "ZEE5"`; this splits the one on
 * the other, so the lead sets small and the destination sets large. It splits
 * ONLY when the label actually ends with the platform name — otherwise the
 * whole label goes on one line and the lead is omitted. Nothing is invented
 * and nothing is dropped, which is the same contract `cut()` and `clauses()`
 * keep on the About movements.
 *
 * <- THE VERB IS NOT "WATCH". Four of the six that render this say "Watch on",
 *   two say "Listen on", and Diwali — which files its link in the Watch
 *   section rather than here — says "View on". The draft hardcoded "Watch on"
 *   because it only ever ran on Aasha. Deriving it is what makes this safe on
 *   the whole route, and a seventh work with a different verb needs no edit.
 *
 * THE TRIANGLE IS SIZED IN em AND THAT IS THE POINT OF ZW13. 0.56em wide by
 * 0.68em tall against the platform word, so it tracks the type at every width
 * with no second breakpoint to keep in step. Measured: a 51.2px word gives
 * 28.7x34.8 and a 32px word gives 17.9x21.8 — 0.56 and 0.68 of the word in
 * both cases. The other thirteen drafts size their marks in pixels and have
 * to restate them on mobile; this one cannot drift.
 *
 * ITS BASELINE IS SET WITH vertical-align AND A SMALL translate, not with a
 * margin: the glyph has to sit on the letters' own baseline, and a margin
 * would move the line box instead of the mark inside it.
 *
 * <- THE ARROW STAYS, AND ON A BARE STILL IT IS LOAD-BEARING. Nothing on these
 *   pages plays: four of the six are bare stills whose opening is `still`, and
 *   pressing this opens the platform in a new tab. A play triangle promises
 *   inline playback, so the arrow is the only mark in the lockup saying the
 *   press goes elsewhere. Do not drop it without replacing what it says.
 *
 * <- AND IT NO LONGER MATCHES `.facade__play`. HANDOFF §5 recorded that the
 *   still's button deliberately copied the facade's — same inset, border,
 *   ground and blur — so that a still which links out and a video which plays
 *   read as one control doing two jobs. That pairing is broken by this change
 *   and the break is deliberate but UNRESOLVED: eight work pages still carry
 *   the pill on their video facade. Either the facade follows this, or the two
 *   controls are meant to differ now because only one of them plays. Flagged
 *   for Ugam; nothing in VideoFacade.astro was touched.
 */
interface Props {
  /** The work's own `external.label`, as written. */
  label: string;
  /** The work's own `external.platform`, as written. */
  platform: string;
  url: string;
}
const { label, platform, url } = Astro.props;

/* Split only on a real match; never manufacture a lead. */
const splits = label.endsWith(platform) && label.length > platform.length;
const lead = splits ? label.slice(0, -platform.length).trim() : '';
const tail = splits ? platform : label;
---

<a class="wm" href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
  <span class="wm__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  {lead && <span class="wm__k">{lead}</span>}
  <span class="wm__p"><i class="wm__tri" aria-hidden="true"></i>{tail}<i
      class="wm__a" aria-hidden="true">&nearr;</i></span>
</a>

<style>
  /* The frame is `position: relative`; this sits in its bottom-left corner at
     the same inset the pill used, so nothing else on the page moves. */
  .wm {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--margin);
    bottom: var(--margin);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 10px;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--bone);
  }
  /* ══ THE LEGIBILITY FLOOR ══════════════════════════════
     ZW13 WORKS ON AASHA'S PHOTOGRAPH AND NOT ON THE CATALOGUE'S, which is the
     one thing fourteen drafts built on one frame could not show. Measured on
     the built site at 1280x800, each ink against the brightest pixel inside
     its own glyph box, floors 4.5:1 under 24px and 3:1 at or over:

       Aasha        lead 14.22  platform  5.02  arrow 17.04  tri 13.77  pass
       Taskaree          5.44             9.82        12.93       9.82  pass
       Ira               5.91             5.06         9.83       6.95  pass
       Special Ops       1.84             2.77         5.45       2.80  FAIL
       Mere Ho           3.06             1.01         1.07      18.09  FAIL
       Ve Maahi          2.95             1.66         2.91       8.90  FAIL

     Mere Ho's platform word reads 1.01:1 — white on white. Special Ops has no
     dark corner to move to: mean .1619, p95 .3376, a blown pixel at .9647,
     and its darkest fifth still peaks at .7396.

     SO THE PLATE COMES BACK, AS A WASH RATHER THAN A PANEL. Ugam's decision,
     2026-10-06, taken over moving the control off the frame.

     0.56 IS SOLVED, NOT CHOSEN. Binary-searched per work against the real
     pixels: Aasha, Taskaree and Ira need 0.000; Ve Maahi 0.269; Special Ops
     0.434; Mere Ho 0.542, which binds. 0.56 is that with a little over.

     <- AND IT MUST BE SOLVED IN GAMMA SPACE, which is the trap here. CSS
       composites alpha per channel on the ENCODED sRGB values, not on linear
       light. Solved in linear light the same six works ask for 0.806 — a
       figure that was quoted once before it was caught, and which would have
       put a black panel on the page to fix a problem that needs a wash. The
       search below composites the way the browser does.

     THE SOFTNESS IS A BLUR ON A FLAT BOX, not a gradient, because a flat core
     is the only shape whose alpha at each glyph can be stated. Every glyph has
     to sit further from the box edge than the blur reaches, or the alpha under
     it is not the alpha solved for.

     <- IT DID NOT, FIRST TIME. At inset 30/40/28/26 with blur(20) the nearest
       glyph measured 19.1px from the edge — 0.9px INSIDE the blur, with this
       comment already claiming it was clear. The contrast still passed, because
       the margins are wide, which is exactly why it would not have been
       noticed. The insets clear the blur by 27.1px at 1280 now.

     <- AND THE PADDING HAD TO STOP BEING PIXELS. Fixed px held their size while
       the lockup shrank, so the scrim grew as a share of the frame: at 375 it
       covered 74.7% of Special Ops, whose 2.1:1 frame is only 155px tall
       there. In em against the platform word it scales with the type, and
       that case fell to 52.5%.

     WHERE IT LANDED, MEASURED ON ALL SIX, BOTH WIDTHS. Every ink clears its
     floor; the figure is the tightest ink on the page as a multiple of it, and
     the share is how much of the frame the scrim covers:

                      1280x800            375x812
       Aasha          3.12x   4.6%        1.78x  19.8%
       Taskaree       2.28x   6.6%        1.96x  28.6%
       Ira            2.34x   6.5%           —      —
       Ve Maahi       1.75x  15.0%           —      —
       Special Ops    1.36x  12.1%        1.44x  52.5%
       Mere Ho        1.07x  15.0%        1.07x  19.5%

     <- TWO THINGS TO WATCH, BOTH UGAM'S CALL IF THEY MATTER. Mere Ho binds the
       whole system at 1.07x — its arrow reads 4.81:1 against a 4.5 floor — so
       a brighter sleeve, or a swapped frame on any of the six, means
       re-solving the alpha rather than assuming it still holds. And Special
       Ops at phone width gives the scrim HALF ITS FRAME, because a 2.1:1
       picture is only 155px tall at 327 wide: the control plus the floor it
       needs is simply large against it. Moving the control below the frame
       under 901px would remove the scrim entirely there, at the cost of a
       second arrangement to maintain. */
  .wm__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    /* em, NOT px, and the font-size here is what makes them resolve against
       the platform word rather than the anchor's 16px. In pixels the padding
       stayed the same size while the lockup shrank, and on Special Ops at 375
       — a 2.1:1 frame only 155px tall there — the scrim covered 74.7% of the
       picture. Tied to the type it scales with it. */
    font-size: var(--wm-size, clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3.2rem));
    left: -0.74em; right: -0.94em; top: -0.70em; bottom: -0.66em;
    border-radius: 0.94em;
    background: rgba(10, 9, 8, var(--wm-scrim, 0.56));
    filter: blur(0.31em);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Positioned, so they paint above the positioned scrim without a z-index. */
  .wm__k {
    position: relative;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bone-dim);
    transition: color var(--d-micro) var(--ease);
  }
  .wm:hover .wm__k { color: var(--bone); }

  /* --wm-size lets a caller step the type down for a smaller frame. The
     default is the size the draft was judged at. */
  .wm__p {
    position: relative;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: var(--wm-size, clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3.2rem));
    line-height: 0.92;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* SIZED IN em SO IT TRACKS THE WORD — see the header. vertical-align sits it
     on the letters' baseline; a margin would move the line box instead. */
  .wm__tri {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 0.56em;
    height: 0.68em;
    background: currentColor;
    clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 50%, 0 100%);
    vertical-align: baseline;
    transform: translateY(0.02em);
    margin-right: 0.22em;
    transition: transform var(--d-micro) var(--ease);
  }
  .wm:hover .wm__tri { transform: translate(0.06em, 0.02em); }

  /* Drawn rather than text-decoration, so it can arrive from nothing and sit
     where this design wants it rather than at the font's underline offset. */
  .wm__p::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: -0.1em;
    height: 2px;
    background: currentColor;
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: left;
    transition: transform var(--d-micro) var(--ease);
  }
  .wm:hover .wm__p::after { transform: scaleX(1); }

  .wm__a {
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 0.34em;
    vertical-align: 0.9em;
    margin-left: 0.34em;
    display: inline-block;
    transition: transform var(--d-micro) var(--ease);
  }
  .wm:hover .wm__a { transform: translate(3px, -3px); }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .wm__tri,
    .wm__p::after,
    .wm__a { transition: none; }
    .wm:hover .wm__tri { transform: translateY(0.02em); }
  }

  /* The inset the pill used below 901px, kept so this change moves nothing
     but the control's own treatment. The mark needs no entry here: it is
     em-sized and follows the word down on its own. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .wm { left: var(--s-3); bottom: var(--s-3); gap: 7px; }
  }
</style>
